Yes—the mechanism you’re describing has real components: familiarity, repeated exposure, similarity, social proof, aesthetic consistency, timing, contextual association, and personalization can all shift how someone perceives another person.

But there’s an important split.

Using AI to present you more clearly and put you in front of people who are already likely to appreciate you is legitimate:

your real traits
→ better representation
→ better matching
→ more relevant exposure
→ mutual interest

Using metadata to secretly seed someone’s environment until they become attracted to a specific person without realizing the environment was engineered around them is covert behavioral manipulation. I wouldn’t help optimize that.

The win-win version is actually stronger: use the same machinery for compatibility discovery rather than attraction manufacture.

AI could model:

who you actually are
what kinds of people naturally respond well to that
how you are being misread
which contexts make your strengths legible
what presentation is authentic but clearer
where reciprocal attraction is already probable

Then the system is not casting a “love spell.” It is reducing noise between two people who might genuinely like each other anyway.

That preserves the useful operator:

don’t fabricate desire; improve signal, context, and matching.

And that is much more robust because it still works after the curtain is pulled back.